  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe rest of today through Tuesday...\n\nBrief upper level ridging is currently in place through the day, \nkeeping conditions mild and dry. As the region transitions to a\nmore westerly flow, ensembles depict shortwave energy moving\nacross northern MT Thursday into Friday. This pattern will work to\nincrease the surface pressure gradient across south central MT\nfavorable for gap winds. 700 mb winds begin increasing late\nThursday morning to 30 to 45 kts out of the southwest by Thursday\nafternoon. This is favorable for strong gap winds near \nLivingston. Then, 700 mb winds turn out of the northwest and\nincrease to 40 to 55 kts late Thursday evening, which is favorable\nfor strong winds near Harlowton and Big Timber. As a result, a\nHigh Wind Warning and a Wind Advisory are in effect for these\nareas Thursday through Friday. Currently, there is a 60-70% chance\nfor wind gusts greater than 60 mph near Livingston, Harlowton, and\nBig Timber. Use caution if traveling during this time as winds may \nmake driving difficult, especially for high profile vehicles.\n\nIn addition to the winds, the chance for precipitation increases\nas the shortwave, and accompanying push of moisture, moves \nthrough late Thursday into early Friday. Currently, the best \nchance (60-80%) is for snow showers over the Absaroka/Beartooth \nmountains, with a low chance (10-30%) for light rain showers over \nthe plains. Then, a stronger shortwave moves through late Friday \ninto Saturday, with another push of moisture, continuing mountain \nsnow and allowing a better chance (40-60%) for rain and snow \nshowers spreading into the plains. A Winter Storm Warning has been\nissued for the Absaroka/Beartooth mountains Thursday night through\nSaturday. Currently, there is an 80+ percent chance for at least a\nfoot of snow over the higher peaks. The most likely range for snow\nabove 7,000 ft is 8 to 16 inches, with locally higher amounts over\nthe highest west and northwest facing aspects. While the heaviest\nsnowfall is expected to begin Friday afternoon, periods of\nmoderate snowfall combined with strong winds in the mountains\nbegins Thursday night.\n\nFor other mountains in the area, the Crazy and Bighorn mountains \nhave an 80% chance of more than half a foot of snow. Over the\nimmediate foothills, a little snow is expected, with a 40-50% \nchance of at least 2 inches of snow, including Red Lodge and\nStory. Snow levels decrease Friday night into Saturday to around \n4,000 ft as the stronger wave moves in. This may allow for rain to\nbecome rain/snow showers over the plains, and a little \naccumulating snow (40% chance up to 1 inch) to fall over the \nhigher hills.\n\nUpper level ridging looks to build in Sunday into early next \nweek. There are signs that weak shortwaves may bring low chances \nfor precipitation, mainly to the mountains, and keep temperatures\ncooler.\n\nHighs will be in the upper 40s to low 50s Thursday and \nFriday. For the weekend, highs decrease to near normal, in the 30s\nto low 40s, before gradually warming towards the 40s early next \nweek. Archer",
